<section #l1-bib-entry | `<bib-entry>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `citations-and-references` · Layer 1 `<bib-entry>` · JATS `<ref (containing element-citation or mixed-citation)>` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `article` `book` `chapter` `thesis` `proceedings` `report` `webpage` `other` | The bibliography entry type. Determines required and optional child elements, and how the entry renders in the bibliography. |

Produced by: *structural — emitted by the compiler, not authored directly*.

</section>

<section #l1-bibliography | `<bibliography>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `citations-and-references` · Layer 1 `<bibliography>` · JATS `<ref-list>` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `style` | kwarg | `author-year` `numbered` `footnote` `custom` | Future — not yet implemented: the interpreter does not read this kwarg or emit data-bibliography-style. Planned: how the bibliography is rendered; typically inherits from the document-level citation-style, with this kwarg overriding for the bibliography rendering specifically. |
| `sort` | kwarg | `alpha` `citation-order` `year` `none` | Future — not yet implemented: the interpreter does not read this kwarg or emit data-bibliography-sort. Planned: how bibliography entries are sorted. "alpha" is alphabetical by author surname (default for author-year styles); "citation-order" is the order in which entries are first cited (default for numbered styles). |
| `type` | kwarg | `cited-only` `full` `hybrid` | Future — not yet implemented: the interpreter does not read this kwarg or emit data-bibliography-type. Planned: which entries appear — "cited-only" includes only entries cited in the document; "full" includes all registered entries; "hybrid" includes cited entries with a separate "Further Reading" section. |

Produced by: *structural — emitted by the compiler, not authored directly*.

</section>

<section #l1-cite | `<cite>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `citations-and-references` · Layer 1 `<cite>` · JATS `<xref ref-type="bibr">` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `keys` | kwarg |  | One or more citation keys, supplied as positional arguments. Multiple keys group into a single citation: <cite key1 key2> renders as a combined citation (e.g., "(Smith 2020; Jones 2021)"). |
| `page` | kwarg |  | Page number or page range for the citation locator. E.g., <cite goodall2024 page=42>. |
| `chapter` | kwarg |  | Chapter reference within the cited work. |
| `section` | kwarg |  | Section reference within the cited work. |
| `prefix` | kwarg |  | Text to appear before the citation. E.g., "see also" in "(see also Smith 2020)". |
| `suffix` | kwarg |  | Text to appear after the citation. |
| `style` | kwarg | `author-year` `numbered` `footnote` `endnote` `inline-author-year` `default` | Override the document-level citation style for this citation. "default" uses the document-level `<citation-style>` from `<config>`. |

Produced by: canonical `<cite>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-cite-1>
The argument is supported by data <cite goodall2024>.
</code>

*Render pending — processSync does not resolve citations — `<cite>` renders empty (issue #273).*

*A simple citation. The resolver renders the citation according to the document-level style. For author-year style, this becomes "(Goodall 2024)". For numbered style, "[1]". The actual rendered text is generated by the citation resolver, not written by the author.*

<code #code:l1-cite-2>
Multiple sources confirm this <cite goodall2024 attenborough2020 darwin1859>.
</code>

*Render pending — processSync does not resolve citations — `<cite>` renders empty (issue #273).*

*Multiple citation keys group into one citation marker. The resolver handles the joining (with separators appropriate to the style).*

<code #code:l1-cite-3>
See <cite goodall2024 page=42> for details.
</code>

*Render pending — processSync does not resolve citations — `<cite>` renders empty (issue #273).*

*A citation with a page locator. The locator renders alongside the citation marker.*

<code #code:l1-cite-4>
See also <cite goodall2024 prefix="cf." page=42-45>.
</code>

*Render pending — processSync does not resolve citations — `<cite>` renders empty (issue #273).*

*Citation with prefix and page range.*

<code #code:l1-cite-5>
A specific work <cite goodall2024 style=footnote>.
</code>

*Render pending — processSync does not resolve citations — `<cite>` renders empty (issue #273).*

*Per-citation style override. This citation renders as a footnote marker even if the document-level style is something else.*

</section>

<section #l1-ref | `<ref>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `citations-and-references` · Layer 1 `<ref>` · JATS `<xref ref-type="...">` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `target` | kwarg |  | The id of the element being referenced. The canonical form uses the #id prefix: <ref #eqn:model>. A kwarg form is also accepted as legacy: <ref target=eqn:model>. IMPORTANT (current implementation): only colon-ids (ids containing a colon, e.g., eqn:model, fig:scatter) are in the label index and can be resolved. Non-colon ids produce an unresolved error marker. This restriction may be relaxed in a future slice. |
| `type` | kwarg | `auto` `figure` `table` `equation` `section` `sub-section` `note` `listing` `theorem` `other` | Author-supplied hint about the intended kind of target. Flows through to a data-ref-type attribute on the rendered anchor, available for CSS/JS to use. The resolver-generated display text is still computed from the id prefix and the DEFAULT_PREFIXES dictionary; this kwarg does not currently override that text. |
| `format` | kwarg | `number` `name` `full` `label-only` `default` | Author-supplied formatting hint. Flows through to a data-ref-format attribute on the rendered anchor, available for CSS/JS. The resolver-generated display text is still computed from the prefix dictionary; future enhancements may use this attribute to vary the rendered text. |
| `+link` | boolean | default `true` | Controls whether the rendered ref is a navigable hyperlink. Default is +link, producing <a href="#targetId" class="ref">...</a>. The -link form produces <span class="ref">...</span> instead — useful when the author wants the ref's display text without making it a navigable anchor (e.g. inside a passage that itself describes the cross-reference rather than invoking it). |
| `+preview` | boolean | default `true` | Controls the hover preview that attaches to the rendered ref by default. Default +preview attaches a tippy tooltip showing the target element's content. -preview adds data-no-preview="true" to the rendered anchor; the hover-preview script's attacher skips elements with that attribute. |
| `+title` | boolean | default `true` | Reserved / unimplemented. The original intent has not been recovered; deliberately set aside until that intent is articulated. Future work, not a current feature. |

Produced by: canonical `<ref>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-ref-1>
See <ref #fig:scatter> for details.
</code>

*Render pending — `<ref #id>` self-declares instead of referencing (issue #268).*

*Canonical form: #id prefix. The ref-resolution plugin replaces the &lt;ref&gt; node with a __ref-marker before hast conversion. The handler then renders an &lt;a&gt; element with computed text. Prefix word is lowercase, from the DEFAULT_PREFIXES dictionary keyed by id prefix.*

<code #code:l1-ref-2>
As shown in <ref #eqn:model>.
</code>

*Render pending — `<ref #id>` self-declares instead of referencing (issue #268).*

*Equation references use the "equation" prefix word by default. Config override: &lt;config ref-prefix-eqn="Eq."&gt; changes this to "Eq.".*

<code #code:l1-ref-3>
<ref #eqn:missing>
</code>

*Render pending — `<ref #id>` self-declares instead of referencing (issue #268).*

*Unresolved target renders a visible error anchor.*

</section>

